A verified example from the same training source follows. It is a different task; implement only the new contract.
Example contract: Input is a nonempty square integer matrix. Return [main diagonal sum,anti-diagonal sum]. A centre cell contributes once to each sum.
Shared domain: Numbers are signed 64-bit integers; all required intermediate and final arithmetic fits i64. Arrays contain at most 64 items and strings are ASCII with at most 256 bytes, unless an explicit task exception says otherwise. Whitespace means space, TAB, LF, FF or CR. JSON booleans and null retain their normal types.
Example solution:
```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    if let Value::Array(matrix) = input {
        let n = matrix.len();
        let mut main_diagonal_sum = 0;
        let mut anti_diagonal_sum = 0;

        for i in 0..n {
            if let Value::Number(num) = &matrix[i][i] {
                main_diagonal_sum += num.as_i64().unwrap();
            }
            if let Value::Number(num) = &matrix[i][n - 1 - i] {
                anti_diagonal_sum += num.as_i64().unwrap();
            }
        }

        Value::Array(vec![
            Value::Number(main_diagonal_sum.into()),
            Value::Number(anti_diagonal_sum.into()),
        ])
    } else {
        Value::Null
    }
}
```
